{"probe_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall.L1.005","canonical_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall","category":"longctx","level":"L1","axis_values":{"context_length":"≤1k","fact_position":"near_question","fact_salience":"explicit_named"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the itinerary and answer the question.\n\n--- ITINERARY (Conference trip, R. Adesina) ---\nTue: Depart SFO 22:15 on flight UA917 to FRA. Wed: Arrive FRA 17:55, train to Heidelberg, check in at Hotel Vier Jahreszeiten. Thu: Conference day 1, keynote 09:30. Reservation Restaurant Schiller 19:30 (party of 4). Fri: Conference day 2, panel at 14:00. Sat: Train back to FRA, depart FRA 11:40 on flight UA918 to SFO.\n--- END ITINERARY ---\n\nQuestion: At what time is the Thursday-evening dinner reservation?\nReply in 24-hour HH:MM format.","ground_truth":"19:30","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"19:30","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_terminal_punctuation":true},"tags":[],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall.L2.001","canonical_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall","category":"longctx","level":"L2","axis_values":{"context_length":"~1066 tokens (chars/4 estimate)","fact_position":"midpoint","fact_salience":"explicit_named"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the following oceanography brief, then answer the question after it.\n\n--- BRIEF ---\nThe Atlantic basin's circulation is dominated by the Gulf Stream system, which carries warm\nsubtropical water along the eastern seaboard of North America before veering eastward near\nCape Hatteras. Smaller eddies — both cyclonic cold-core and anticyclonic warm-core — calve\noff the meander and persist for months, transporting heat, salt, and biological tracers\nacross thermal boundaries. Researchers have used satellite altimetry since the early 1990s\nto track sea-surface height anomalies that correlate with eddy cores; the SARAL/AltiKa and\nSentinel-3 missions provided particularly fine resolution after 2013. In the deep ocean,\nmoored arrays such as the RAPID-MOCHA line at 26.5°N continuously measure the strength of\nthe Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation. Time series from RAPID indicate a long-term\nweakening trend whose significance against natural variability remains debated. Cold-water\nformation in the Labrador and Nordic seas is thought to be sensitive to freshwater fluxes,\nand ice-core proxies suggest the AMOC has switched between modes during glacial terminations.\n\nA 2024 reanalysis combining Argo float profiles with satellite altimetry estimated\nthat warm-core ring W2-07's transport across the 36°N latitude line peaked in week 9\nof its life at approximately 1.8 sverdrups; weekly averages over the rest of its\nlifetime were lower. Comparable cold-core rings showed peak transports in the range\n0.4 to 1.1 sverdrups.\n\nThe Atlantic basin's circulation is dominated by the Gulf Stream system, which carries warm\nsubtropical water along the eastern seaboard of North America before veering eastward near\nCape Hatteras. Smaller eddies — both cyclonic cold-core and anticyclonic warm-core — calve\noff the meander and persist for months, transporting heat, salt, and biological tracers\nacross thermal boundaries. Researchers have used satellite altimetry since the early 1990s\nto track sea-surface height anomalies that correlate with eddy cores; the SARAL/AltiKa and\nSentinel-3 missions provided particularly fine resolution after 2013. In the deep ocean,\nmoored arrays such as the RAPID-MOCHA line at 26.5°N continuously measure the strength of\nthe Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation. Time series from RAPID indicate a long-term\nweakening trend whose significance against natural variability remains debated. Cold-water\nformation in the Labrador and Nordic seas is thought to be sensitive to freshwater fluxes,\nand ice-core proxies suggest the AMOC has switched between modes during glacial terminations.\n\nSubsequent work by the Halifax group used inverse modelling to reconcile shipboard ADCP\ntransects with the altimetric estimates; they argued the ring's net heat flux was\ndominated by the upper 800 m and that intrusions of slope water modulated the signal.\nPapers in this area frequently cite the difficulty of separating ring-driven transport\nfrom background Gulf Stream meanders.\nThe Atlantic basin's circulation is dominated by the Gulf Stream system, which carries warm\nsubtropical water along the eastern seaboard of North America before veering eastward near\nCape Hatteras. Smaller eddies — both cyclonic cold-core and anticyclonic warm-core — calve\noff the meander and persist for months, transporting heat, salt, and biological tracers\nacross thermal boundaries. Researchers have used satellite altimetry since the early 1990s\nto track sea-surface height anomalies that correlate with eddy cores; the SARAL/AltiKa and\nSentinel-3 missions provided particularly fine resolution after 2013. In the deep ocean,\nmoored arrays such as the RAPID-MOCHA line at 26.5°N continuously measure the strength of\nthe Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation. Time series from RAPID indicate a long-term\nweakening trend whose significance against natural variability remains debated. Cold-water\nformation in the Labrador and Nordic seas is thought to be sensitive to freshwater fluxes,\nand ice-core proxies suggest the AMOC has switched between modes during glacial terminations.\n--- END BRIEF ---\n\nQuestion: According to the brief, what was the estimated peak-week transport of warm-core ring W2-07,\nin sverdrups?\nOutput only the number.","ground_truth":"1.8","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_numeric","expected":1.8,"tolerance":0.0001,"unit":"Sv","require_unit":false,"accepted_output_format":"numeric_only","require_only_numeric_output":true},"tags":["long_context","fact_retrieval","numeric_recall","position_invariant"],"notes":"Peak-week transport now stated as 1.8 Sv. Answer 1.8 Sv."}
{"probe_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall.L2.002","canonical_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall","category":"longctx","level":"L2","axis_values":{"context_length":"~1136 tokens (chars/4 estimate)","fact_position":"midpoint","fact_salience":"explicit_named"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the following contract excerpt. After it, answer the question.\n\n--- AGREEMENT (excerpt) ---\nThis Master Services Agreement ('MSA') is entered into as of March 3, 2025, by and between\nHelix Cartography, Inc. ('Helix') and Berenson Logistics LLC ('Berenson').\n\nSection 4. Confidentiality. Each Receiving Party shall hold the Disclosing Party's Confidential\nInformation in strict confidence and shall not disclose such information to any third party without\nthe prior written consent of the Disclosing Party, except to its employees, contractors, and\nprofessional advisors who have a need to know and who are bound by obligations of confidentiality\nno less restrictive than those contained herein. The obligations under this Section shall survive\ntermination of this Agreement for a period of five (5) years, except with respect to trade secrets,\nwhich shall be maintained in confidence for so long as such information remains a trade secret\nunder applicable law. Nothing in this Section shall prevent disclosure required by law, court\norder, or governmental authority, provided that the Receiving Party gives prompt written notice\nto the Disclosing Party and reasonable cooperation in any effort to seek a protective order.\n\nSection 5. Term. This Agreement shall commence on the Effective Date and continue for an\ninitial term of three (3) years, automatically renewing for successive one-year terms unless\neither party provides written notice of non-renewal at least ninety (90) days before the\nend of the then-current term.\n\nSection 6. Fees. Berenson shall pay Helix a monthly recurring fee of $42,500, invoiced on\nthe first business day of each calendar month and due net thi