{"probe_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall.L2.005","canonical_id":"longctx.position_invariant_recall","category":"longctx","level":"L2","axis_values":{"context_length":"~944 tokens (chars/4 estimate)","fact_position":"midpoint","fact_salience":"explicit_named"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the following historical essay excerpt and answer the question after it.\n\n--- ESSAY EXCERPT ---\nThe medieval salt trade across the Bay of Biscay was carried in single-masted nefs and, later,\nin the larger holk-style vessels that began appearing in northern ports during the fourteenth\ncentury. Records from Bayonne customs, surviving only in fragments, suggest the volume of salt\narriving from the Bourgneuf bay rose steadily through the 1340s before collapsing during the\nplague years. Bristol merchants are recorded as buyers in the Bordeaux wine fleets and as\noccasional resellers of Iberian salt downstream into the Welsh coastal towns. Notarial archives\nin San Sebastián preserve a handful of contracts naming Castilian masters who shipped wool to\nthe Low Countries on the return leg, a triangular pattern that anticipated later Atlantic trades.\nThe exact tonnages are difficult to reconstruct: tax accounts list values in pounds Bordelaise,\nin livres tournois, and occasionally in maravedís, and exchange rates fluctuated.\n\nA late-fifteenth-century cog known by the name 'Maria de Lekeitio' is referenced in three\nseparate sources: a 1471 customs entry at La Rochelle, a 1473 contract preserved in the\nBilbao notarial archive, and an undated portolan note. The Bilbao contract specifies\nthat her master, one Juan de Arizaga, agreed to deliver 144 lasts of salt to Bruges by\nthe feast of Saint Martin.\n\nThe medieval salt trade across the Bay of Biscay was carried in single-masted nefs and, later,\nin the larger holk-style vessels that began appearing in northern ports during the fourteenth\ncentury. Records from Bayonne customs, surviving only in fragments, suggest the volume of salt\narriving from the Bourgneuf bay rose steadily through the 1340s before collapsing during the\nplague years. Bristol merchants are recorded as buyers in the Bordeaux wine fleets and as\noccasional resellers of Iberian salt downstream into the Welsh coastal towns. Notarial archives\nin San Sebastián preserve a handful of contracts naming Castilian masters who shipped wool to\nthe Low Countries on the return leg, a triangular pattern that anticipated later Atlantic trades.\nThe exact tonnages are difficult to reconstruct: tax accounts list values in pounds Bordelaise,\nin livres tournois, and occasionally in maravedís, and exchange rates fluctuated.\n\nBy the early sixteenth century the pattern of trade had shifted. Antwerp's rise as a\ntransshipment hub drew commerce away from the older Flemish ports, and English wool\nexports flowed increasingly through the Merchant Adventurers' staple. In the Cantabrian\nports themselves, shipbuilding capacity was redirected toward the Indies trade after\n1500.\n\nThe medieval salt trade across the Bay of Biscay was carried in single-masted nefs and, later,\nin the larger holk-style vessels that began appearing in northern ports during the fourteenth\ncentury. Records from Bayonne customs, surviving only in fragments, suggest the volume of salt\narriving from the Bourgneuf bay rose steadily through the 1340s before collapsing during the\nplague years. Bristol merchants are recorded as buyers in the Bordeaux wine fleets and as\noccasional resellers of Iberian salt downstream into the Welsh coastal towns. Notarial archives\nin San Sebastián preserve a handful of contracts naming Castilian masters who shipped wool to\nthe Low Countries on the return leg, a triangular pattern that anticipated later Atlantic trades.\nThe exact tonnages are difficult to reconstruct: tax accounts list values in pounds Bordelaise,\nin livres tournois, and occasionally in maravedís, and exchange rates fluctuated.\n--- END ESSAY ---\n\nQuestion: According to the essay, who was the master of the 'Maria de Lekeitio'?\nReply with the name only, exactly as it appears.","ground_truth":"Juan de Arizaga","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"Juan de Arizaga","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_terminal_punctuation":true},"tags":[],"notes":""}
